00:02:59.366 --> 00:03:01.940
So what are our drivers
and benefits here?

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.870
What drives us to do this?

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:10.240
We need a single source of
truth across multiple domains.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.050
So what is single
source of truth?

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:15.910
So because we have many
databases, like any other shop,

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.255
we have db2 running on
mainframe and Oracle

00:03:18.255 --> 00:03:19.880
running in different
location, and then

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.420
each database and each
type has its own data,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.220
and we need a single source.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.940
That's one of the major
drive to go for the Striim.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.410
And also, so the
second one is to reduce

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.800
the mainframe, maintenance,
and license cost, of course.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.155
So there's operational
cost of the mainframe.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:44.750
And speed to market
impacted data,

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.190
so residing in multiple
different systems.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.530
So what does that
mean is, we have data

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.250
residing in multiple databases.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.858
And then if you need to fast
develop the application,

00:03:54.858 --> 00:03:57.150
so you need to reach out to
the different applications,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.440
and then different areas to
develop your application.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.950
Now we are making
everything to single source,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.790
and then everything available
in the Google Cloud.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.100
And also we want to make
sure the different customer

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.780
experience across
different systems,

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.780
because we don't want a customer
field in the online customer

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.440
fields different in the store.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.880
So that's the main
mode of omnichannel.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.329
All the customer face
the same experience.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.640
And finally, but
not last, so this

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.280
is the inability to sunset
existing legacy systems.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.940
So some of the legacy systems,
if you need to sunset,

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.450
so we need to migrate that data
to some other place like Cloud,

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.820
so this Striim is helping us.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.490
So this is our
architectural diagram.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.613
So it's still very high level.

00:04:43.613 --> 00:04:45.530
So here I'm going to
take a little longer time

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.090
to explain what we
are doing, and what

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.520
we can expect from here.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.710
So if you see on the
left side of the diagram,

00:04:54.710 --> 00:05:00.870
that is our Macy's on-prem data
centers where the data exists.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.510
So we have inventory data.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:05.120
It exists in the mainframe db2.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.010
So db2 on mainframe.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.550
So that's the Oracle databases.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:16.880
So that data is replicating
using Striim to the GCP Cloud.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.460
So that is the Cloud
Spanner and also Pub/Sub.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:26.600
So what Striim does here,
right, so Striim runs an app

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.560
so that will read
the source data.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.260
So Striim app contains multiple
tables, one or many tables,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.750
which contains [INAUDIBLE]
the source data

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:42.060
and [INAUDIBLE] the target.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.880
So you can come
capture and apply,

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:47.490
so you can collect
[INAUDIBLE] and push.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.510
So that's what Striim
is doing right now.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:54.670
So if you focus on the
middle part of the Striim,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:06:00.860
so we are calling as a VM
and suppose VM1 and VM2.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.010
So we are running
two VMs in the GCP.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:08.000
So we are running two
VMs because search

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.230
for high availability purpose.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.670
So if an app runs
these VMs and one VM

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.450
goes down for any reason, for
the maintenance of anything,

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.660
apps automatically will load
the other VM seamlessly.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.990
And then we don't
see any impact,

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.476
there is no business impact.

00:06:26.476 --> 00:06:29.720
And so that's the
reason I would recommend

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.390
at least two VMs for
the [INAUDIBLE] server

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.820
to minimizing outages.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:40.700
So Striim [INAUDIBLE] is running
in GM, so in the GCP VMs.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.990
And then, that data is pushed to
the Spanner, and also Pub/Sub.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:48.920
And also, it came to the BQ,
so [INAUDIBLE] Striim is,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.860
so it can apply all three
or all two at the same time.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.900
So you don't need to
depend on each other.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.370
So now how are we doing this?

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.390
So Striim saw on on-prem
without running a GoldenGate.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.340
So Striim has the ability to
read GoldenGate trail files,

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:14.240
then convert it in [INAUDIBLE]
use of the target site.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.970
So that's a long thing.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:17.650
So then, if you
see those options

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.540
the online migration phases.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.160
So there we mentioned two steps.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.600
One is initial load, and the
other one with log-based CDC.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.050
What is initial load?

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.530
So when I create a new
table at the target

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:33.950
location in the Cloud
Spanner, so the game is empty.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.170
So we did source data.

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.060
So it's like a flat file.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.400
So the Striim app can
read the flat file

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.150
and then play like
[INAUDIBLE] So

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.420
let's say so if I
do at the time T1,

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:52.120
so I took the flat
file from the source.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.850
And then I apply all the
data into the target.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:58.930
After two hours, that
could count two time given,

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.190
plus source's time T2.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:05.110
So time T2 are both source
and target are in sync.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.630
So but what happens, the
changes that are happening

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:10.060
between time T1 and T2.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.110
Because this is like
a flowing river.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.870
So data flow never stops.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:20.480
So what we do is so we take
the time T1 minus five minutes.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.650
So to go back to the time,
and then we run in real time

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:27.880
CDC, so using the stream.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.290
So we're going to a
five minutes overlap.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.690
But we don't miss any data.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.720
So that data will apply.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.020
All the duplicate data that
happens at the target site

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:41.150
because that data might exist
because of the five minutes,

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.059
and then Scanner will ignore it.

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:49.620
So after catch up the real
time, so they're not at time T3,

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.140
both source and target
are near real time.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.607
So that is very critical
for our business.

00:08:55.607 --> 00:08:57.190
But whatever happens
changes happening

00:08:57.190 --> 00:09:00.460
at the source data business in
the DB2 Mainframe or Oracle.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.570
The applications that are using.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:07.660
So the customer is accessing
data, using GCP apps,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.510
using Google Apps.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:14.500
So they should get the real
data and the real time.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.910
So data [INAUDIBLE]
in near real time.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:23.020
So in our shop, so when we did
this, from source to target

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.678
takes 200 to 300 milliseconds.

00:09:25.678 --> 00:09:27.220
So when you talk
about this, you need

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.930
to talk about that
network speed,

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.700
you need a lot of
tuning parameters.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.560
So each shop is different.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.880
But that's what we achieved.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.670
So that's one of the
major requirements for us.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:43.550
So here I want to talk
about why Cloud Spanner.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.530
Because we have many databases.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.360
And so, we have so many options.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:50.710
Why Cloud Spanner?

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:54.010
So we need a consistency
of the inventory data

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.870
from source and target.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.350
And also, so the accessing
data is supposed to be faster.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:05.320
So we have so many databases,
everything [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:09.760
So standard stays on top of it.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.720
So then when you
look at the options,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.710
so Spanner fills
our requirements.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:22.100
And also, most importantly, as
a DBA, as a database person, I--

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:25.220
one of the requirements
is high availability

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:27.500
and disaster recovery.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:32.790
So by definition, Spanner
can span multiple regions

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.480
so that will satisfy
multi-region.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.590
So that satisfies the
disaster recovery.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:41.090
In case one region goes
down, other two regions

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:43.542
can automatically take over.

00:10:43.542 --> 00:10:45.480
And also high availability.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.250
So if one region goes
down, other region

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.350
is always available for us.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:54.700
But, also transactions can
be accessed from anywhere.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.580
So across the globe.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.260
But for us at Macy's,
we [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.130
across the United States.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:02.720
Some can access
from Chicago, some

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.580
can access from North Carolina,
some can access from Texas.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:11.190
And if were the third option,
that is a relation system.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:16.421
So this is also one of the
requirements we're looking for.

00:11:16.421 --> 00:11:19.790
So relational, because all
our source databases, all

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:21.920
our transactional
source databases,

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.230
are relational systems.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:27.950
So it's easy to transfer
the applications and systems

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.360
from source to target.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.310
So if you have a similar
or same kind of support.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:36.230
So this is full fledged
relations system, Spanner.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.200
So like I mentioned before,
so this is the real-time data

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.460
responses.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:49.020
And the responses are
faster, and then data sharing

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.160
will happen in the Spanner.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:55.760
So that also meets
our requirement.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:00.180
Now, so the last point
is need for ability

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:03.140
to scale to meet
spikes in demand.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.100
So this is one of
the key requirements

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.240
for the retail industry,
because retail business is not

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.480
same like any other
day if you compare it

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.650
to Black Friday
and Cyber Monday.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.630
Those peaks are very high.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:20.800
So Spanner is easily scalable,
horizontally scalable.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:28.230
And so, during the peak time,
that solves all our capacity

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:32.540
issues, and also user
experience doing holidays time.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:38.170
Next.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:43.000
So this is architecture,
why Striim?

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.720
So we have many application
tools available.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.260
So it's in the market.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:49.180
So why Striim?

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:50.760
Right?

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.660
So Striim has a highly available
real-time replication system.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.144
So I can send near
real-time replication.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:05.920
Also, Striim has proven the low
latency end-to-end replication,

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:08.620
so that takes
milliseconds to reach

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.810
from source to the target.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:15.710
Like I mentioned before, that is
very critical for our business.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:18.510
A need for
enterprise-grade features

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.540
like high availability,
recoverability,

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:25.300
ability to handle
volumes and velocity.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:26.900
So we talked about
high availability,

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:32.450
that is for requirement for
any tool or any process we use.

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:35.720
And also, I believe it will
depend on value and velocity.

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:39.040
So this is, again, a critical
point for the retail industry.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:45.970
When you see during holidays,
this is a very needed point

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:49.240
because our balance go
up, our speed goes up,

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:51.300
a lot of transaction goes up.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:55.190
So Striim is supporting that.

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:59.180
And these requirements also lead
to the need for CDC solution.

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.650
So we have application,
we have so many ways.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:06.050
But, Striim, once in the CDC,
so that is a change in data

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:12.050
capture solution, which
is very proven technology,

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:16.340
not as fast, as it won't put
a lot of stress or resource

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.120
constraints at the source level.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.680
And on the next one
is need for ability

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.550
to write multiple targets.

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:28.360
So this is important,
too, because if you're

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.670
publishing your
data with Spanner,

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:34.130
and then same data may need
to consume by the Pub/Sub,

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:35.490
and also BigQuery.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.290
So you don't need to
depending on the data

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.870
to come into the Spanner
then right into the Pub/Sub,

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:42.060
from there go to BigQuery.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:46.900
Now, Striim, one app, can
run into multiple targets.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.060
Sometimes all of
these targets can

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:52.220
use for different use cases.

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.010
But we can attribute to
one app from the Striim.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:58.730
So there's an added advantage.

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:04.620
So the final point is
complement existing investment.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.300
So what does that mean?

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:14.130
That means we already invested
the money on this GoldenGate.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.950
So GoldenGate data is
already extracting the data

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.410
and then replicating
to different databases.

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:23.850
So [INAUDIBLE] we are invested
the energy and money on it.

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:28.060
So now Striim has the capability
to read the GoldenGate type

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:34.500
files, which is complement
existing investments.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.660
So what does that mean?

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:41.310
So here at Macy's, so we are
already using an application

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:43.900
to GoldenGate,
[INAUDIBLE] GoldenGate,

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.690
which is replicating data
from the source database

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:52.700
to the target database, from
IBM mainframe to Oracle.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:58.400
So Striim has a capability of
reading GoldenGate type files,

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.530
and they transform the
data or replicate that data

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:05.700
into the GCP, Spanner,
Pub/Sub, and BigQuery.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:12.750
So we just achieved the process.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:16.380
So without investing
another CDC process,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:22.040
or EMQ kind of process, so
this complement the existing

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:23.240
investment.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.870
[INAUDIBLE] So that helps us.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.168
So that concludes
my presentation.

00:16:29.168 --> 00:16:33.300
And so I know this
is not completed.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:34.680
This is not complete picture.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.158
You may have some questions.

00:16:36.158 --> 00:16:37.700
If you have any
questions, and if you

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:40.240
need to understand more
deep how we did it,

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:42.380
and what we need to get it done.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:44.730
So I'm happy to answer
those questions.

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.800
So thank you, and just
hand it over to Alok.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:52.270
ALOK PAREEK: Thank you, Neel.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:53.860
Thanks very much.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.800
And particularly, I wanted to
mention a couple of points.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:01.690
One was, of course, that during
peak period when you guys had

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:05.800
the 7,500 transactions per
second, the end to end latency

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:09.790
to keep the solution real time,
200, 300 milliseconds as you

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.880
pointed out, I think
that was really

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:16.250
good for trying to modernize.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.700
And the other part of course
was leveraging the existing

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:21.880
investment, and I was
the CTO for GoldenGate,

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:25.660
so I'm happy that you were
able to take Striim, which

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.710
is sort of a
next-generation platform,

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.670
and complement that with
your existing investment.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:34.030
So with that I'm going to get
into the details of the Striim

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:34.820
back on itself.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.600
As Neel pointed out,
there's multiple solutions

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:42.050
that Striim enables.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.680
At broad, higher level
category, there's

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.530
Cloud adoption, hybrid
Cloud data integration,

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:49.370
and in-memory Striim processing.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:53.370
So let me step into
each one of these.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.000
So Striim is the
next-generation platform.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.450
What you're seeing
here is a layout.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:00.940
So on the left
side, you have what

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:02.810
I call your on-premise systems.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.830
These could be your current
systems, your legacy systems,

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:08.290
and they could also be your
best-of-breed applications

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:11.520
that are running on
some other Cloud.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.080
And on the right-hand side
are the newer endpoints.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:17.200
So as you're modernizing
your own business,

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.490
as you're introducing
newer systems,

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:22.360
be they be Cloud-based,
relational database

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.600
as a service, or most equal
systems, or storage systems,

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.680
or analytic systems, oftentimes
you want to bridge this gap.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:32.530
And one of the key
things is if you

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:35.320
take a look at trying to
keep things consistent

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:40.060
and real time, traditional
technologies don't always

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.730
meet up to that goal, and
there's several reasons

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.170
I want to share.

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:45.250
OK?

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.310
So the way to look at
and interpret this slide

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:50.350
is going from the
left to the right,

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.200
you need a next
generation modern platform

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.480
that allows you to
deploy these solutions.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:58.360
And with that, there are
nine different flavors.

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:00.890
I don't have the time to
go into each one today,

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:03.640
but I certainly want to
touch on a few of them which

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:06.320
are relevant to what
Neel talked about.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.590
So the first one
is cloud adoption.

00:19:09.590 --> 00:19:11.470
So these have to
do with today you

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:16.720
might be deploying your existing
database systems on-premise

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:19.570
and you want to simply
migrate to the cloud

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.090
because you want to take
advantage of, let's say,

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:25.930
a database in a managed
fashion to lower your cost,

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.630
to make it a lot
more manageable,

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:30.850
as well as to achieve
the economies of scale

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:34.270
from a compute perspective,
a storage perspective.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:37.330
So that migration,
how do you do that

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:41.230
without interrupting your
own business operations?

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.270
And one of the very
interesting solutions

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:46.510
here is to keep your
current application, which

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:48.940
is running against
your legacy system,

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:53.260
up and running while you set up
a parallel system in the Cloud.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:57.490
And so long as you're able to
set that up and keep the two

00:19:57.490 --> 00:20:00.550
systems synced up
and concurrently

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:02.410
running, then at
some point, you're

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:04.690
able to transition
over your application

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:05.740
onto the target site.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.880
So this is what I mean
by the one-way migration.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:14.740
More interestingly, in the case
of the use case that we just

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.540
heard about, what
they wanted to do

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:20.500
was take a legacy system,
in this case, I mainframe,

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.290
and take a lot of that
data and move that

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:26.650
into a modern system
like Cloud Spanner.

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:28.900
So when you do that, then
you need to be able to,

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:30.200
again, replicate that data.

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:31.990
So that's the
replication scenario.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:34.990
And similarly there's
also requirements

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:38.080
that come up as you're trying
to distribute your data.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:40.780
So you also saw that there
was in Neel's architecture

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:43.670
slide, that was a
Google Pub/Sub endpoint.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:46.270
So that's a data
distribution use case.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:48.220
As we try to liberate
our data, as we

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:50.230
try to modernize
our applications,

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:52.390
different parts
of an organization

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.690
have their application
development teams, their data

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:58.030
scientists, their
partners, and they all

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.070
want to have access
to this data.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.930
Traditionally this
has been problematic

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:05.470
because it is very expensive
to collect and ingest

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:07.630
the real-time data that's
going against a very

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:09.500
mission-critical system.

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:14.500
And that is one area
where the overall solution

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:17.740
of doing a one-time initial
load followed by a change data

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:19.690
capture allows you
to effectively meet

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:20.710
that requirement.

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:21.430
OK?

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:22.950
So just generalizing
forward, I'm

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.210
going to go a
little faster here.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.640
A generalization of the
previous solution category

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:29.690
is the hybrid cloud
data integration.

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:31.450
So this has to do
with the fact that you

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:33.940
may want to do not just
a one-way migration,

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:40.870
but do a kind of a synchronized
migration where you're running,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:44.470
let's say, system A on-premise,
you're replicating it to system

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:48.520
B, then you do some
testing on system B,

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:52.690
possibly migrate a few users,
do a reverse topology where

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:55.540
the data is coming back
into the legacy system

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:57.370
from the Cloud based
system, and now you're

00:21:57.370 --> 00:22:00.220
synchronizing these two,
and ultimately at some point

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:02.710
you're going to pull
the plug on one of them.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:08.920
Real-time data integration, big
data integration, a lot of you

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.770
are concerned with doing
data leak type of projects

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:14.050
where you're trying to take
advantage of Cloud storage

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:15.260
systems.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:17.500
How do you get data
into those tiers?

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:19.400
So that's the second
category here.

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:20.840
And finally, the third one.

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:25.100
This is truly the gateway
towards digital transformation.

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:27.940
So how do you deploy
next-generation

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:34.770
advanced very thin apps so that
they can be deployed almost

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.480
in a micro services fashion?

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.440
Just to give you an example, as
you're moving the data, if you

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.880
are trying to count
up things to make sure

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.370
that operationally
things are adding up,

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:49.180
or if you want to alert on
them if things are not what

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.210
you expect.

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:53.550
So these are what I call the
streaming applications that

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:57.690
can be also deployed to
deliver to the business

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:02.100
the ability to monitor the
operations very closely,

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:05.610
as well as if you want to get
any insights into the data

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:06.990
specifically.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:11.340
For example, let's say if
you're talking about order data.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.440
Am I exceeding the
number of orders

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:14.910
within a given time interval?

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:17.670
And should someone be
alerted in the journey of?

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:21.480
Now, you can always do this
by writing an application

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.900
on the target endpoint for
example in Google Spanner.

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.163
But for real time
purposes, oftentimes we

00:23:27.163 --> 00:23:29.580
have found that people may
want to do that in the pipeline

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:30.020
instead.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:30.510
OK?

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:31.635
So that's this third piece.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:40.270
So our focus is really
on customers like Macy's.

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:45.760
And it's typically the global
1,000 types of customers.

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:52.180
Typically, these customers have
a very, very strong requirement

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:53.690
as far as scale is concerned.

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:56.080
So their data volumes
are rather large.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:59.060
And they may range
anywhere from order

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:03.400
of 10s to 50s of
terabytes to let's

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:06.190
say as low as 50 gigabytes.

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:08.110
It doesn't have to
be, but typically we

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:11.020
see that the stream
solution is being adopted

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:13.910
in these kinds of environments.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:16.450
And the one fundamental
thing that I really

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.260
wanted to call out
was oftentimes,

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:21.910
there's a reluctance
to modernize

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:25.340
because there's an impact
to your actual application.

00:24:25.340 --> 00:24:27.520
So let's say your
CRM application,

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:29.020
or your core
banking application,

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:32.390
retail application, inventory
management application.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.370
So how do you then
modernize that system

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:40.960
by making the actual
technology that's

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:43.900
moving the data non-intrusive
to the functioning

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.010
of that application?

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:47.060
That's really where we focus on.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:47.590
OK?

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:51.040
So why do traditional data
integration technologies

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:52.660
fall short here?

00:24:52.660 --> 00:24:55.860
And there's a number
of different reasons.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:59.110
So number one, they
often are batch-oriented

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:01.660
and have a job
mindset, which means

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:05.740
that typically you run
some sort of an ETL job

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:07.210
and then that gets done.

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:11.350
And now you have taken data that
was different from last night,

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:14.770
and now you move that
into the target system.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:20.482
This can be done nightly.

00:25:20.482 --> 00:25:22.190
But that doesn't give
you real-time data.

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.430
So that's really one
fundamental challenge there.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.990
Number two is just lack of
support for data streaming.

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:31.480
So if you wanted to give
real-time visibility,

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:34.030
and you're operating off of
a reporting system that's

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:35.710
reflecting data
as of last night,

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:38.050
then there is no
possible way in which

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:42.170
you could do what
Macy's was able to do,

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:47.230
which was to deliver consistency
across a system like Spanner.

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:48.700
Number three,
scalability limits.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.260
So you want to
have a system that

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:52.480
can scale up with your volumes.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:56.050
So to that end, Striim
is a distributor platform

00:25:56.050 --> 00:25:57.970
that can scale horizontally.

00:25:57.970 --> 00:26:00.040
Number four, and this is
a very innovative thing

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.890
we have done in a traditional
data integration stack

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:06.820
by adding capabilities
like data validation.

00:26:06.820 --> 00:26:09.310
It's one thing that you
move a finite amount of data

00:26:09.310 --> 00:26:11.440
and you're done, and now
you can do comparisons.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.450
It's another where the
data flow will never stop.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:17.440
It's going to on an
ongoing basis, keep moving.

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.870
And so how do you ensure that
the source and the target

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:22.507
systems are behaving as
you expect them to behave?

00:26:22.507 --> 00:26:24.340
So that's this whole
area of data validation

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:26.740
that we have added
into the platform.

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:31.300
And lastly, if you wanted to
incorporate advanced things

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:35.320
like machine learning your
own models into the pipeline,

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:37.000
those are also
interesting things

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.810
that customers want to do, and
traditional integration stacks

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:41.890
fail to accommodate
for that flexibility

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:43.330
that the platform desires.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:46.330
So within Striim,
what we have done

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:53.950
is we made the provisioning,
the designing, the deploying,

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:58.760
the monitoring of these
pipelines very, very simple.

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:02.800
And the way you do that is by
using the flow designer, which

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:07.060
allows you to connect to a
number of source systems,

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:09.970
and you can design
your data pipeline,

00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:13.970
and then you can deliver that
data into a target system.

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.450
So in this case, what
Neel talked about was

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:19.120
there would be a DB2 or
an Oracle GoldenGate,

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:21.520
or an Oracle, say,
system on the left side.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:24.550
On the target, you would have
a system like Google Pub/Sub

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:26.140
and Cloud Spanner.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.390
And you could move the data.

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:30.760
And along the way,
the interesting thing

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.580
is that you are able to
do SQL-based processing

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:34.940
on that data.

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:37.210
So you can literally
take a look at a record,

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.800
and you're able
to filter on that.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:43.090
You could have a very wide
record format with 400 fields

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:45.520
and you could say, I want
only 80 of these fields.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:46.790
You could transform that.

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:50.290
So if you had personally
identifiable records,

00:27:50.290 --> 00:27:51.880
you could mask
those along the way.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:57.460
You could do aggregation by
running SQL, doing a group buy,

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:00.985
and condensing the record, and
moving it to a target system.

00:28:00.985 --> 00:28:02.860
You could enrich the
data, and you could also

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:03.740
correlate the data.

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:05.050
So these are the capabilities.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:06.842
I don't have the time
today to go deep dive

00:28:06.842 --> 00:28:07.850
into each one of these.

